<!--
    arraysubfield: a partial access into a plain array-typed symbol must
    report the size it actually touches.

    P9 emission.  `PrintC::pushSymbolDetail` routes a Varnode that covers only part
    of its mapped Symbol through `pushPartialSymbol`, whose type walk descends
    struct/union/array members uniformly and, when no member describes the access,
    emits the artificial `._[off]_[size]_` field.  kuna's port routed STRUCT and
    UNION into that walk but split plain ARRAY off into a dedicated `name[index]`
    branch that computes `index = symboloff / elementAlignSize` WITHOUT consulting
    the access size.  So an 8-byte read at offset 0 of a `char[16]` rendered
    `g[0]`, a one-byte element, and an 8-byte read at offset 8 rendered `g[8]`,
    the ninth byte.  Both claim a size the program does not use.

    Fix: route plain TYPE_ARRAY through the partial-symbol walk too.  The walk's
    own ARRAY arm carries the upstream `TypeArray::getSubEntry` guard
    (newoff + size fits inside one element), so a genuine in-element access still
    renders its subscript and only a span across elements falls to the artificial
    field.  Correctness fix, no option: it restores upstream's own rendering, so it
    is a Convergences row in docs/history.md, not a divergence.

    Source (gcc -O2 -no-pie, x86-64):

        char g[16];
        unsigned long lo8(void)  { unsigned long v; memcpy(v, g, 8);     return v; }
        unsigned long hi8(void)  { unsigned long v; memcpy(v, g + 8, 8); return v; }
        char          elem(void) { return g[3]; }
        unsigned __int128 shift64(unsigned long x) { return (unsigned __int128)x LSH 64; }

    Bytes verbatim from that build: lo8@0x401130, hi8@0x401140, elem@0x401150,
    shift64@0x401160, g@0x404040 (.bss, 16 zero bytes so the RIP-relative loads
    have readable backing).  `map address` plants the typelocked char[16] the
    strings/data analysis plants on a real image.

    lo8/hi8 pin the repair on a global array; elem pins that a one-byte access at
    offset 3 KEEPS its `g[3]` subscript; shift64 is the reported reproducer
    (interp-bee `eval_result_noun` @ 0xa3e0), a 16-byte register-pair return whose
    two 8-byte halves are written into an `undefined1[16]` container.  The
    shift line of shift64 is NOT fixed here: the function is still not compilable
    C.  This test is about the access size, assertion #7 pins the part that
    survives; it reads `v1._0_16_ << 0x40` rather than the upstream bare
    `v1 << 0x40` because `arraycoverwidth` (default on) also states the width of
    a WHOLE-array cover; the shift of an array member is what remains wrong.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x401130">
f30f1efa488b05052f0000c30f1f4000f30f1efa488b05fd2e0000c30f1f4000
f30f1efa0fb605e82e0000c30f1f4000f30f1efa4889fa31c0c3
</bytechunk>
